Introduction
Plastic and plastic-wrapped materials are extensively utilized in commodity production due to their low cost, high plasticity, and corrosion resistance, which cater to the vast needs of human daily life. Currently, the primary types of plastics include polyethylene, polyvinyl chloride, polyester, polypropylene, and polystyrene (Agboola and Benson, 2021; Wang et al., 2020; Allsopp and Vianello, 2000; Dris et al., 2017). During and after their use, plastics undergo size reduction due to their physical properties. The widespread distribution of these tiny plastic units has raised significant concerns regarding their potential impact on human health and the environment. Consequently, the term ‘microplastics’ (MPs) has emerged, typically referring to plastic microfibres, microbeads, fragments, nurdles, and foams with sizes less than 5 mm (Thompson, 2004). Further classification defines plastic particles between 1 nm and 1 μm as nanoplastics (NPs) (Frias and Nash, 2019). These particles accumulate in the environment, undergo transportation and transformation, and ultimately pose a risk of accumulation in the human body (Su et al., 2021).
The issue of MPs was initially reported in the marine environment (Thompson, 2004). However, subsequent research has revealed that the extent of MPs pollution on land far exceeds that in marine environments (Nizzetto et al., 2016). Whether in commercial areas with dense populations, remote uninhabited regions, or even in Antarctica, MPs are severely threatening local biodiversity and disrupting ecological balance (Obbard et al., 2014; Barnes et al., 2010). Consequently, public awareness of the dangers posed by MPs is increasing, leading to a growing body of research focused on this pressing issue.
The natural degradation of MPs is an extremely prolonged process, involving mechanisms such as photooxidative degradation, thermal degradation, ozone degradation, catalytic degradation, mechanochemical degradation, and biodegradation (Manzoor et al., 2022). Due to the inherent stability of MPs and various environmental constraints, natural degradation is exceedingly difficult, necessitating urgent artificial intervention. Currently, the primary methods for MPs removal can be categorized into physical, chemical, and biological approaches (Huang et al., 2022a; Lapointe et al., 2020; Zhang et al., 2020), with biological methods being more promising and practical than the other two. However, given that each approach has its limitations, integrated treatment methods that combine two or more techniques are often preferred.
This review summarizes the aforementioned reports, compares related processes, and provides references for future research directions. In the following sections, we will delve deeper into the origins and pathways of MPs, analyzing their environmental distribution and impact on both terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems. We will then explore the current methodologies for MP detection and quantification, highlighting their strengths and limitations. Finally, we will examine the most recent advances in MP remediation technologies, with a particular focus on biological approaches, and discuss the challenges and opportunities for future developments in this field. By providing a comprehensive overview of these aspects, this review aims to contribute to a better understanding of the complex issue of MP pollution and to guide future research and policy-making efforts in addressing this global environmental challenge (Table 1, Table 2, Table 3).
